00:08:56.208 --> 00:08:56.750
DI DANG: Yes.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:58.770
MARK MANDEL: What is that?

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:00.480
DI DANG: That is
a great question.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.272
Let's kind of break
that down a little bit.

00:09:02.272 --> 00:09:03.510
So the first question, AI--

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:05.960
so AI is a superset
of technologies

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:07.790
that include machine learning.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:11.000
When I say human
centered AI, it means

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:13.370
how are we being
cognizant of our role

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:17.510
as product creators when it
comes to creating AI systems,

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:20.780
or AI driven products, or even
working on machine learning.

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:22.700
Because the truth of
it is that there's

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:25.190
a lot that we, as
designers and developers,

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:28.910
intentionally and
unintentionally influence

00:09:28.910 --> 00:09:31.170
through all the phases
of machine learning,

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:33.740
whether that's in the
kind of training data

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:38.180
that we collect and label,
to how we actually build,

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:40.910
and train, and evaluate our
machine learning models,

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.580
to ultimately, the kind
of output and predictions

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:45.110
that come from these models.

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.690
We, as people, are affected
by those predictions,

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:49.050
by those results.

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:51.710
So human centered AI means how
can we make sure that we're not

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:54.020
just focused on the
technology of AI,

00:09:54.020 --> 00:09:55.940
but we're asking
questions around

00:09:55.940 --> 00:09:58.023
how it impacts us as people
at the end of the day.

00:09:58.023 --> 00:09:59.565
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Yeah, I think we've

00:09:59.565 --> 00:10:01.190
seen a little bit
coming up recently,

00:10:01.190 --> 00:10:03.980
a greater understanding
of data bias.

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:05.270
And this is a big part of--

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:06.650
because obviously,
when you train

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:08.240
any form of machine
learning, or AI,

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.790
or whatever it is, it's based
on the data you give it.

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:12.110
And I think a lot of
people now are starting

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.402
to understand a little bit
more about what data bias is

00:10:14.402 --> 00:10:16.730
and how easy it
is to kind of skew

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:19.367
what an AI system
will kind of generate.

00:10:19.367 --> 00:10:21.200
MICHELLE CASBON: Yeah,
oh, yeah, absolutely.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.533
So one of the things that we
talk about in the guidebook

00:10:23.533 --> 00:10:24.920
is looking at rater UI.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:27.202
So raters refer to
teams with individuals

00:10:27.202 --> 00:10:29.410
who are often doing the
labeling of the training data

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:31.040
sets themselves.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:34.250
And when we, say, like give
a simple task to a rater

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:38.480
to, say, label a set of images,
as is this a running shoe

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:41.570
or not a running shoe,
what are their connotations

00:10:41.570 --> 00:10:42.553
around what that means?

00:10:42.553 --> 00:10:43.970
So for instance,
if we showed them

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:46.280
a data set that
includes sneakers,

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:50.033
high heels, stilettos,
fancier sneakers, et cetera,

00:10:50.033 --> 00:10:51.450
what are they
labeling accordingly

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:52.130
and what are they not.

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:53.850
That's kind of a
lower stakes example.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.990
If I were to give a higher
stake example of, say, an image

00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:00.290
data set of label who
identifies as a woman here,

00:11:00.290 --> 00:11:01.850
or what is female.

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:03.530
That's where it gets
really complicated

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:06.680
around data bias and the
own cultural implications

00:11:06.680 --> 00:11:09.125
that these raters may be
bringing to that task.

00:11:09.125 --> 00:11:10.500
Because if you
say, for instance,

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:14.600
you have in that photo set an
image of like RuPaul, who's

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:16.580
a drag queen, or
Jonathan Van Ness,

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:21.470
from Queer Eye, individuals
who identify as male,

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:23.840
but may dress in more
androgynous ways or more

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:26.330
non-binary ways, if you
get that set of images

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:28.950
to a rater who's based
in another country,

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:30.700
another culture, what
kind of connotations

00:11:30.700 --> 00:11:32.283
are they going to
be bringing to that.

00:11:32.283 --> 00:11:34.190
And then that can skew
the kind of results

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:36.740
that you have in the end, if
you had like an image classifier

00:11:36.740 --> 00:11:38.823
model around male and
female, for whatever reason.

00:11:38.823 --> 00:11:40.448
MARK MANDEL: And just
a quick shout-out

00:11:40.448 --> 00:11:42.080
as well, episode
114 of the podcast,

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:44.447
we did a machine learning
bias and fairness episode,

00:11:44.447 --> 00:11:46.280
where we dig into this
a whole lot more too.

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:48.620
Not to say that we shouldn't
talk about it now of course.

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:49.875
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Although
one point for talking about it

00:11:49.875 --> 00:11:52.670
now is you mentioned a
couple of times a guidebook.

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:53.560
DI DANG: Yes.

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:56.060
Maybe we should have started
with that, seeing as how that's

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:57.240
one of the main reasons, yeah.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:58.040
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I think
it's interesting to talk

00:11:58.040 --> 00:11:59.210
about the deep
stuff, but what is

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:00.502
the guidebook, what does it do?

00:12:00.502 --> 00:12:01.840
DI DANG: Yeah, right.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:04.990
So backing up a little bit,
the People Plus AI Guidebook,

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:07.180
we'll be releasing that
at I/O. I'm guessing

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:08.740
this will be live by then.

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:12.130
But we'll be launching at
I/O. And this is an effort

00:12:12.130 --> 00:12:15.310
that teams across Google
have been developing

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:18.250
over the past year, chiefly
spearheaded by PAIR,

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:20.743
so again the People
Plus AI Research Team.

00:12:20.743 --> 00:12:22.660
And essentially, it came
out of this question,

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:24.580
as different product
teams were wrestling

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:27.400
with what are the best
practices for designing

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:30.610
with AI, what design
patterns work, don't work?

00:12:30.610 --> 00:12:33.400
How do you establish
trust with the user?

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:36.520
How do you convey the importance
of a confidence interval

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:39.093
and how the user should act
on that or not act on that?

00:12:39.093 --> 00:12:40.510
All these different
questions that

00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:43.688
arose around creating
AI products at Google,

00:12:43.688 --> 00:12:45.230
kind of took a step
back and thought,

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:49.510
well, why not synthesize that
in a set of guidance or best

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:52.667
practices that we can share
with everyone, and that,

00:12:52.667 --> 00:12:54.250
again, going back
to our earlier point

00:12:54.250 --> 00:12:56.350
around these nascent
technologies, where

00:12:56.350 --> 00:12:58.600
the patterns or
the best practices

00:12:58.600 --> 00:12:59.968
are still in development.

00:12:59.968 --> 00:13:02.260
How can we share this resource
out there, get feedback,

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:03.635
from everyone in
the world, who's

00:13:03.635 --> 00:13:05.560
wrestling with
these same problems,

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:09.850
and eventually work towards
a healthier, more beneficial,

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:11.710
inclusive set of
design principles.

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:12.960
MARK MANDEL: So you solved it.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:14.832
You solved it, it's fine now?

00:13:14.832 --> 00:13:16.415
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Once it's published.

00:13:16.415 --> 00:13:17.498
MARK MANDEL: So it's done.

00:13:17.498 --> 00:13:18.753
No [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:18.753 --> 00:13:19.420
DI DANG: I wish.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:21.110
It's a continual
work in progress.

00:13:21.110 --> 00:13:22.692
And that's one of
the reasons why--

00:13:22.692 --> 00:13:23.650
that's why I came here.

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:26.548
Because it's a project that
I believe very strongly in,

00:13:26.548 --> 00:13:28.465
but it's not like we
just release it at that's

00:13:28.465 --> 00:13:29.248
the end of that.

00:13:29.248 --> 00:13:30.790
One of the things
that I'm working on

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:33.100
as a design advocate
is how can we

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:34.930
keep an open channel
of communication

00:13:34.930 --> 00:13:37.150
between the PAIR team
that's developing

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:39.730
this content and the
rest of the world?

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:41.860
And what best
practices or patterns

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:44.530
may need to be localized
for certain cultures?

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:46.940
Are there specific
needs that we're seeing

00:13:46.940 --> 00:13:48.550
in different communities?

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:51.730
Or are folks more interested
in health care, fintech,

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:53.408
or whatever guidance?

00:13:53.408 --> 00:13:55.450
So that's why-- that's a
big reason why I'm here,

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:58.360
is not just to share the news
about this great resource

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.240
that developers and product
creators can utilize when it

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:03.140
comes to creating
AI driven products,

00:14:03.140 --> 00:14:05.770
but also to hear from
everyone out there,

00:14:05.770 --> 00:14:08.253
what's useful about the
guide book, what's not,

00:14:08.253 --> 00:14:09.670
what's missing,
what's inaccurate,

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:12.320
and how can we make it
better to better serve you.

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:15.580
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So who's your
main target audience for this?

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:17.400
If you had to pick
the ideal person,

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:19.600
and you're like I wish
they would read this,

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:20.675
who are those people?

00:14:20.675 --> 00:14:22.300
DI DANG: I'd say the
target audience is

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:25.880
anyone who's considering
working with AI driven products.

00:14:25.880 --> 00:14:28.000
To put a finer point
on that, the guidebook

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:30.460
is written so that
it's very accessible

00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:32.450
and that has a low
barrier to entry.

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:35.022
It's written primarily for
UXers and product managers,

00:14:35.022 --> 00:14:37.480
because the truth of it is,
there aren't a lot of resources

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:41.880
out there around AI driven
product decisions for UXers

00:14:41.880 --> 00:14:42.660
and PMs.

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:45.490
You have a lot of great
stuff like TensorFlow,

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:47.680
you have MLC from Google.

00:14:47.680 --> 00:14:50.110
But those are more
engine developer focused.

00:14:50.110 --> 00:14:52.420
And so we wanted to make
sure that we were offering

00:14:52.420 --> 00:14:55.347
a variety of resources
that everyone could use,

00:14:55.347 --> 00:14:57.430
that your cross-functional
product team could use,

00:14:57.430 --> 00:14:59.358
to help answer these
questions together.

00:14:59.358 --> 00:15:01.150
Another way of thinking
about the guidebook

00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:03.800
is that it's not a crash
course in machine learning.

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:06.040
It's not intended to be
technical by any means.

00:15:06.040 --> 00:15:08.890
But it's intended to
help you make decisions

00:15:08.890 --> 00:15:10.818
around AI driven products.

00:15:10.818 --> 00:15:12.360
MARK MANDEL: Correct
me if I'm wrong,

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:15.640
it sounds almost like
before you had this,

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:17.770
unless you were
technical, it was probably

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:20.350
very hard to understand what's
happening and that's a risk.

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:22.600
Like if you've got people
who are building products,

00:15:22.600 --> 00:15:24.883
who don't really understand
how the technology works,

00:15:24.883 --> 00:15:26.300
because the resources
don't exist.

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:28.580
Like, there's potential
for things to go wrong.

00:15:28.580 --> 00:15:30.080
DI DANG: Yeah, oh,
yeah, absolutely.

00:15:30.080 --> 00:15:33.550
And a lot of it's also because
the field of machine learning

00:15:33.550 --> 00:15:34.360
has--

00:15:34.360 --> 00:15:35.780
it's been around
for decades now.

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:38.080
And in the last
decade or so, it's

00:15:38.080 --> 00:15:39.940
been continually
advanced by developers,

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:41.890
by folks who are more
engineering focused,

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:43.790
data scientists, et cetera.

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:47.140
And it's only recently that
product managers, UXers,

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:49.013
designers, are
realizing that we need

00:15:49.013 --> 00:15:50.680
to be a part of these
conversations too,

00:15:50.680 --> 00:15:53.290
so that we can make
decisions about the product

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:55.098
for our users, as a team.

00:15:55.098 --> 00:15:56.890
After all, you have a
cross-functional team

00:15:56.890 --> 00:15:59.740
that's composed of
PM, engine designer,

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:01.960
for any other
application or product,

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:05.670
why not also for machine
learning or AI driven products?

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:08.352
Because that's not something
that we see as much right now.

00:16:08.352 --> 00:16:10.060
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
it sounds like it'll

00:16:10.060 --> 00:16:11.830
be changing over
time, especially

00:16:11.830 --> 00:16:13.510
as you work with these
different people.

00:16:13.510 --> 00:16:15.177
Ideally, anyone who's
hopefully using it

00:16:15.177 --> 00:16:17.020
is also able to help
contribute towards it,

00:16:17.020 --> 00:16:19.180
and say, these are the
thoughts that I have.

00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:20.613
But for people
who don't know it,

00:16:20.613 --> 00:16:22.780
this is a great way to get
started or at least start

00:16:22.780 --> 00:16:24.130
to get some considerations.

00:16:24.130 --> 00:16:25.780
What exactly is the guidebook?

00:16:25.780 --> 00:16:28.340
Is it like a big O'Reilly book?

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:30.840
Are we talking about a couple
of pages, is it a white paper?

00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:31.520
MARK MANDEL: Is it a story book.

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:32.440
Does it have lots of pictures?

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:33.070
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
That would be great.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:34.330
DI DANG: I wish,
oh, man, maybe that

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:35.320
could be the next iteration.

00:16:35.320 --> 00:16:36.690
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Guidbook
V2, pop-up version.

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:39.370
DI DANG: If we keep getting
enough feedback on our feedback

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:41.470
form on the site
then surely I can

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:43.387
advocate for it to become
a pop-up story book.

00:16:43.387 --> 00:16:44.928
MARK MANDEL: You
heard it here first.

00:16:44.928 --> 00:16:46.570
Make sure you put in
there pop-up story

00:16:46.570 --> 00:16:47.860
book for the next version.

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:49.777
DI DANG: And when I see
that feedback come in,

00:16:49.777 --> 00:16:52.640
it's like that's how I know,
you listen to the GCP podcast.

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:54.040
Thank you for calling.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:56.530
So the form factor,
it's a microsite.

00:16:56.530 --> 00:16:58.522
And it's composed
of six chapters.

00:16:58.522 --> 00:17:00.730
Let me see if I can rattle
off from memory right now.

00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:03.670
The first chapter is user
needs and defining success.

00:17:03.670 --> 00:17:06.190
And so that chapter helps
you answer earlier questions

00:17:06.190 --> 00:17:09.130
around, should we AI
this, should we ML this?

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:10.980
What are good uses cases for AI?

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:11.930
What are not?

00:17:11.930 --> 00:17:14.200
When would the user or
even your product team

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:15.700
benefit from using AI?

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:17.589
When would you not, et cetera?

00:17:17.589 --> 00:17:19.948
The next is data
collection and evaluation.

00:17:19.948 --> 00:17:21.490
So again, Mark, to
your earlier point

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:25.000
about how can we be cognizant
of implicit and explicit biases

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:28.480
that we may be bringing into
the collection, labeling,

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:29.950
evaluation of our data.

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:32.500
The next chapter
is mental models.

00:17:32.500 --> 00:17:35.170
And that looks at what
is the mental model

00:17:35.170 --> 00:17:38.110
that your user is bringing
to this application,

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:39.850
to this experience.

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:42.670
The general public, there's a
lot of wildly varying opinions

00:17:42.670 --> 00:17:46.090
around what is AI, and
confusion with that, and robots,

00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:48.460
or whatever other things.

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:51.340
So how can you better
understand the assumptions,

00:17:51.340 --> 00:17:54.970
expectations that your user
is bringing to your product?

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:57.820
And then adjust the design or
the experience of your product

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:00.500
accordingly to match
those expectations.

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:03.070
The fourth chapter looks at
exploitability and trust.

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:06.790
Given that you may have
varying degrees of transparency

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:10.000
into the predictions that
your AI system is making,

00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:13.180
how can you then convey
that level of transparency

00:18:13.180 --> 00:18:14.320
to the user as a result?

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:15.460
What is useful to them?

00:18:15.460 --> 00:18:16.018
What is not?

00:18:16.018 --> 00:18:17.560
Also takes a look
at, as we mentioned

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:20.410
earlier, confidence levels,
in terms of sometimes

00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:22.810
you need to be more transparent
with the user in terms

00:18:22.810 --> 00:18:25.300
of the degree of confidence
they should be investing

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:26.520
in this application.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:28.520
The next chapter looks
at feedback and controls.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:32.230
So this is a key aspect to any
kind of application creation,

00:18:32.230 --> 00:18:35.260
in terms of how can the
user tweak their settings?

00:18:35.260 --> 00:18:37.990
How can they provide
feedback into the system,

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:40.630
to then train the model
to better suit their needs

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:43.120
and be better personalized
to their desires?

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:46.150
And then last chapter is
errors and graceful failure,

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:48.675
acknowledging the simple truth
that your model will fail.

00:18:48.675 --> 00:18:50.300
There will be some
error at some point.

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:53.000
So how do you move the user
forward in spite of that?

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:53.750
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:18:53.750 --> 00:18:55.815
So how do people
use this guidebook?

00:18:55.815 --> 00:18:57.190
Obviously, there's
a pop-up story

00:18:57.190 --> 00:19:00.010
that they have to read,
but outside of that,

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:01.442
what should they
be doing with it?

00:19:01.442 --> 00:19:03.400
DI DANG: So it's intended
as a reference guide.

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:05.950
So if you find that you
are struggling with issues

00:19:05.950 --> 00:19:07.990
in any of those chapters,
you can pull it up,

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:09.370
can read through
certain sections

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:12.280
to answer any questions that
may be cropping up on your team.

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:15.910
In the guidebook, there are
also vignettes or mockups

00:19:15.910 --> 00:19:17.920
of a fake app called Run.

00:19:17.920 --> 00:19:20.050
These mockups, these
vignettes, capture

00:19:20.050 --> 00:19:24.580
kind of patterns to aim
for or patterns to avoid.

00:19:24.580 --> 00:19:26.980
And these are all
based on real insights

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:28.920
from product teams
across Google.

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:31.468
We've just generisized it for
the purposes of the guidebook.

00:19:31.468 --> 00:19:33.010
You can take a look
at that and learn

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:35.110
from those example patterns.

00:19:35.110 --> 00:19:36.820
And also, in the
guidebook there is

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:39.630
a worksheet that is attached
to every single chapter.

00:19:39.630 --> 00:19:42.130
I know that makes it sound like
homework, a little less fun.

00:19:42.130 --> 00:19:43.720
But the idea of the
worksheet-- it's

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:45.820
kind of a shorter
reference guide.

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:49.780
Includes a couple of checklists,
pointers, different questions

00:19:49.780 --> 00:19:52.210
and exercises that you
can pose to your team,

00:19:52.210 --> 00:19:53.710
to come to these
decisions together.

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:55.127
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So you mentioned

00:19:55.127 --> 00:19:56.830
a big part of your
role specifically

00:19:56.830 --> 00:19:58.300
is going around
and communicating

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:00.925
with these people, getting their
feedback, and then, of course,

00:20:00.925 --> 00:20:02.810
I hope folding that
back into the guidebook.

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:04.990
Who has really been
providing a lot of feedback?

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:06.850
And are people,
maybe, even using

00:20:06.850 --> 00:20:09.250
this guidebook, or at least
concepts from this guidebook

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:10.640
without ever having
receiving it?

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:12.890
DI DANG: Well, we started
development on the guidebook

00:20:12.890 --> 00:20:14.590
almost a full year ago.

00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:17.353
And there's over 30
or 40 individuals

00:20:17.353 --> 00:20:19.270
from different product
teams, across Alphabet,

00:20:19.270 --> 00:20:21.400
across Google, who have
contributed their insights

00:20:21.400 --> 00:20:22.700
or learnings to this.

00:20:22.700 --> 00:20:25.400
And we've also been internally
testing the guidebook

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:27.400
with a few different
product teams across Google

00:20:27.400 --> 00:20:28.765
in the last couple of months.

00:20:28.765 --> 00:20:30.640
Like I said, we'll be
launching the guidebook

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:32.680
at I/O. We have a
40 minute session

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:34.210
talk dedicated to guidebook.

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:36.340
And to help bring
these concepts to life,

00:20:36.340 --> 00:20:39.100
we'll be illustrating it through
a case study from the Google

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:39.642
Flights team.

00:20:39.642 --> 00:20:41.558
So that's one of the key
teams that we've been

00:20:41.558 --> 00:20:42.717
piloting this content with.

00:20:42.717 --> 00:20:44.800
MARK MANDEL: So, I'm sure
that our listeners would

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:48.160
love to hear more about the
particular best practices that

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:49.690
have come out of the guide.

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:52.660
Is there maybe one or
two that are particularly

00:20:52.660 --> 00:20:53.285
your favorites?

00:20:53.285 --> 00:20:54.743
DI DANG: One of
the concepts that's

00:20:54.743 --> 00:20:57.100
been most interesting to me,
and also, in sharing this

00:20:57.100 --> 00:20:59.380
out with people both in
and outside of Google,

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:01.360
is looking at the
cost of optimizing

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:02.662
for precision or recall.

00:21:02.662 --> 00:21:04.870
And this is something that
I'm still like-- each time

00:21:04.870 --> 00:21:06.190
I talk about it, I
want to make sure I

00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:07.450
get the terminology right.

00:21:07.450 --> 00:21:10.375
Because I believe precision is
optimizing for false positives

00:21:10.375 --> 00:21:12.250
and recall is optimizing
for false negatives,

00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:14.050
or it's the other way around.

00:21:14.050 --> 00:21:15.568
Each time, I feel like look--

00:21:15.568 --> 00:21:17.860
I feel like I actually have
to sketch out the quadrants

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:19.902
and look at it to make
sure I'm getting it right.

00:21:19.902 --> 00:21:23.110
But the short of it is that
we need to design the reward

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:26.280
function in a way that
optimizes for one or the other,

00:21:26.280 --> 00:21:28.255
but we need to strike
the fine balance.

00:21:28.255 --> 00:21:30.130
And I think an interesting
case study of this

00:21:30.130 --> 00:21:31.643
is with the Google
Clips team, which

00:21:31.643 --> 00:21:34.060
is it that that little wearable
camera that you could wear

00:21:34.060 --> 00:21:35.410
on your shirt and that
would automatically

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:36.850
capture little
clips of your day,

00:21:36.850 --> 00:21:38.850
and that you could then
sort through at the end.

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:41.370
And that way, it frees
you up to live your life.

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:42.880
You don't have to
be behind a lens.

00:21:42.880 --> 00:21:44.170
But you have
something that's kind

00:21:44.170 --> 00:21:45.962
of capturing these
special moments for you,

00:21:45.962 --> 00:21:49.510
that later on, you can decide
to keep or dispense with.

00:21:49.510 --> 00:21:51.700
And so an interesting
example of this concept

00:21:51.700 --> 00:21:54.190
of optimizing for precision
or recall come to life

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:56.410
is with the Google Clips team.

00:21:56.410 --> 00:21:58.960
They realized through the
process of user research

00:21:58.960 --> 00:22:02.490
that actually, users didn't
want the camera technology

00:22:02.490 --> 00:22:04.890
to optimize too
much for precision,

00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:08.310
in terms of like what the camera
deemed as the most interesting

00:22:08.310 --> 00:22:10.530
or the most unique as a photo.

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:12.060
Users prefer the
Google Clips camera

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:14.280
to optimize more
for recall, in terms

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:16.297
of like a wider
diversity of pictures,

00:22:16.297 --> 00:22:17.880
even if it's something
that they don't

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:19.290
deem inherently interesting.

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:21.450
Because it gave them
a sense of control

00:22:21.450 --> 00:22:23.970
to be able to sift through
their gallery and choose what

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:25.670
was actually special to them.

00:22:25.670 --> 00:22:28.170
And in the end, they felt like
they could have greater trust

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:30.087
in the Google Clips
camera, because it allowed

00:22:30.087 --> 00:22:32.580
them to define what was
actually special to them,

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:35.280
and make sure that no special
moment was being left out.

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:37.980
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well, in that
case, there's such a low cost.

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:39.605
Because at the end
of the day, the user

00:22:39.605 --> 00:22:41.590
basically says no, I
don't want this picture.

00:22:41.590 --> 00:22:44.220
But in a more complex
scenario, that might have that,

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:46.650
there's got to be other balances
that take effect, right?

00:22:46.650 --> 00:22:48.900
DI DANG: Yeah, so another
interesting case study to me

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:50.850
comes from the
Google Brain team.

00:22:50.850 --> 00:22:52.830
When they built a
deep learning model

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:56.400
that could diagnose diabetic
retinopathy in patients.

00:22:56.400 --> 00:22:58.590
And I can't remember why
now, but for some reason,

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:00.540
there's a variety of
confounding factors

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:02.803
that make it difficult
for physicians

00:23:02.803 --> 00:23:05.220
who are highly trained and
experienced in this to diagnose

00:23:05.220 --> 00:23:05.925
this.

00:23:05.925 --> 00:23:08.550
And so, they actually found that
through the course of creating

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:11.460
this model, instead
of automating away

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:14.550
the human physician
diagnosis, the model,

00:23:14.550 --> 00:23:16.260
working in tandem
with physicians,

00:23:16.260 --> 00:23:19.980
could actually result in
higher accuracy of diagnosis

00:23:19.980 --> 00:23:22.870
than either the model or
the physician by themselves.

00:23:22.870 --> 00:23:24.810
And so that's an
interesting example to me

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:28.230
of how we think about
using AI for automation

00:23:28.230 --> 00:23:30.690
or for augmenting the human
ability to do something,

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:32.910
like striking that fine
balance between both.

00:23:32.910 --> 00:23:34.920
And that's something else that
we also cover in the user needs

00:23:34.920 --> 00:23:36.240
and defining success chapter.

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:38.448
MARK MANDEL: That sounds
like a really core component

00:23:38.448 --> 00:23:40.810
of literally what you
call human centered AI.

00:23:40.810 --> 00:23:44.910
Like you want to make sure that
it's an augmenting experience

00:23:44.910 --> 00:23:46.382
in some way, shape, or form.

00:23:46.382 --> 00:23:47.340
DI DANG: Yeah, exactly.

00:23:47.340 --> 00:23:49.980
When we are making a decision--
if we, as product creators,

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:53.170
are making a decision
to automate away a task,

00:23:53.170 --> 00:23:55.710
are we crystal clear that we're
meeting the ideal criteria

00:23:55.710 --> 00:23:56.670
for automating it away?

00:23:56.670 --> 00:23:58.800
So for instance,
it makes less sense

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:02.940
to automate a task that humans
find pleasurable, or enjoyable,

00:24:02.940 --> 00:24:05.190
or even higher stakes,
it makes less sense

00:24:05.190 --> 00:24:08.638
to automate away a task
that people can't agree on.

00:24:08.638 --> 00:24:10.930
Because then if we can't
agree on the way to do a task,

00:24:10.930 --> 00:24:13.597
that it means that we don't have
a clear set of criteria to then

00:24:13.597 --> 00:24:14.430
train the model on.

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:16.930
There's also an interesting--
so kind of going deeper here--

00:24:16.930 --> 00:24:18.120
Mark, to your point earlier.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:21.450
There's an interesting question
around cultural bias or value

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:24.270
here, where some of the early
feedback that I've heard

00:24:24.270 --> 00:24:26.700
is when we make
assumptions about tasks

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:29.920
that are onerous or
tedious to automate away,

00:24:29.920 --> 00:24:32.700
that maybe that's a more
Western way of looking

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:33.678
at doing something.

00:24:33.678 --> 00:24:35.220
We're in a greater
level of privilege

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:37.680
to be able to say, look,
actually I don't feel like--

00:24:37.680 --> 00:24:39.900
I don't know-- opening my
mail, or scanning for spam,

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:40.955
or what have you.

00:24:40.955 --> 00:24:42.330
But that's a
decision that we can

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:44.830
make due to our relative
level of privilege.

00:24:44.830 --> 00:24:46.890
So what are the
implications of when we

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:48.480
do agree to automate something?

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:51.220
What does it say about
our value as a society?

00:24:51.220 --> 00:24:52.720
Is that a decision
that we're making

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:55.780
or imposing on another culture
that we should be aware of?

00:24:55.780 --> 00:24:57.280
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Well, I'm not sure

00:24:57.280 --> 00:24:59.207
if anybody enjoys
opening junk mail, but--

00:24:59.207 --> 00:25:00.540
DI DANG: Yeah, that was a more--

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:01.980
MARK MANDEL: Some people might.

00:25:01.980 --> 00:25:02.820
Find your joy.

00:25:02.820 --> 00:25:04.153
MARK MIRCHANDANI: It should be--

00:25:06.170 --> 00:25:07.910
besides the
meditative experience

00:25:07.910 --> 00:25:10.000
of finding out what
sparks joy in you,

00:25:10.000 --> 00:25:11.810
does this guidebook
also address that,

00:25:11.810 --> 00:25:14.163
like the cultural
segmentation almost,

00:25:14.163 --> 00:25:15.830
that comes up from
some of these things.

00:25:15.830 --> 00:25:18.170
Because it seems to me like it
would be really hard to make,

00:25:18.170 --> 00:25:20.060
exactly what you said, a
blanket statement, that says,

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:21.750
look, everyone agrees
on no one wants to--

00:25:21.750 --> 00:25:22.970
DI DANG: If it's tedious,
no one wants to do it.

00:25:22.970 --> 00:25:25.580
Yeah, but there's monetary
value in doing certain tasks,

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:27.395
like those are
some people's jobs.

00:25:27.395 --> 00:25:28.520
So being cognizant of that.

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:30.145
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Well, that's probably

00:25:30.145 --> 00:25:34.520
the biggest kind of global
reaction to a lot of AI,

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:36.350
is that, hey, it's
stealing jobs.

00:25:36.350 --> 00:25:39.050
And we just talked about
how augmentation is really

00:25:39.050 --> 00:25:40.712
a little bit more
of the goal there.

00:25:40.712 --> 00:25:42.170
What's the response
to those people

00:25:42.170 --> 00:25:43.550
who are exactly saying that?

00:25:43.550 --> 00:25:44.360
Like, hey--

00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:46.942
DI DANG: Yeah, I
honestly don't know.

00:25:46.942 --> 00:25:47.650
I don't have an--

00:25:47.650 --> 00:25:49.880
I wish I had an answer for
you on that, but I don't know.

00:25:49.880 --> 00:25:51.680
I think it's something
that we're all still

00:25:51.680 --> 00:25:53.600
figuring out together.

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:56.210
And for me, in launching
this guidebook,

00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:59.270
I want to make sure that
we are launching this

00:25:59.270 --> 00:26:00.530
in events around the world.

00:26:00.530 --> 00:26:02.300
That we're reaching out
to developer and design

00:26:02.300 --> 00:26:03.530
communities around the world.

00:26:03.530 --> 00:26:05.030
Because I think
it's very, very easy

00:26:05.030 --> 00:26:08.450
to speak to, exclusively
West Coast audiences,

00:26:08.450 --> 00:26:09.943
or even Western audiences.

00:26:09.943 --> 00:26:12.110
And we need to understand,
from design and developer

00:26:12.110 --> 00:26:16.550
communities as far away as like,
say Russia, Brazil, Slovakia,

00:26:16.550 --> 00:26:18.330
et cetera, does this
resonate with you?

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:21.170
Does this capture how you think
about AI or machine learning?

00:26:21.170 --> 00:26:22.778
And if not, what is missing?

00:26:22.778 --> 00:26:23.570
What is inaccurate?

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:27.788
How does it need to grow in
order to better serve you?

00:26:27.788 --> 00:26:30.080
MARK MANDEL: I'm assuming
the same thing applies again,

00:26:30.080 --> 00:26:33.230
like socioeconomic, status,
gender, like a whole bunch

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:35.720
of different diversity
aspects that make up basically

00:26:35.720 --> 00:26:38.530
society as a whole really kind
of affect how you're going

00:26:38.530 --> 00:26:39.955
to make these AI models work.

00:26:39.955 --> 00:26:42.330
DI DANG: Yeah, again, it's
not just about the technology.

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:45.050
It's about the impact the
technology has on us as people.

00:26:45.050 --> 00:26:47.000
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
is it possible to make

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:49.840
an unbiased model?

00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:50.660
MARK MANDEL: Whoa.

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:51.560
DI DANG: I don't think so.

00:26:51.560 --> 00:26:53.727
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Welcome
to the deeper group, where

00:26:53.727 --> 00:26:55.090
we go deep into questions.

00:26:55.090 --> 00:26:56.840
DI DANG: I feel like
I need like a drink--

00:26:56.840 --> 00:26:58.070
I feel like we all need
drinks for this right now.

00:26:58.070 --> 00:26:59.278
Even the listeners right now.

00:26:59.278 --> 00:27:01.820
MARK MIRCHANDANI: This might
be a beers conversation.

00:27:01.820 --> 00:27:02.903
DI DANG: I don't think so.

00:27:02.903 --> 00:27:04.280
I mean, speaking
personally here.

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:08.270
Bias is a natural part
of what makes us human.

00:27:08.270 --> 00:27:10.820
But when I say natural, there's
not a value judgment there

00:27:10.820 --> 00:27:13.010
of it being good
natural or bad natural.

00:27:13.010 --> 00:27:16.340
It's something that we need to
be cognizant of and aware of.

00:27:16.340 --> 00:27:19.880
And we're not just having
reflexive, knee jerk reactions.

00:27:19.880 --> 00:27:22.970
This is a big part of my
overall design and product

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:24.680
philosophies, that
we need to be very

00:27:24.680 --> 00:27:27.170
conscientious of the decisions
that we make as product

00:27:27.170 --> 00:27:29.810
creators, because the
decisions we make around,

00:27:29.810 --> 00:27:31.520
say, like
accessibility, or where

00:27:31.520 --> 00:27:33.410
this particular element
of UI is located,

00:27:33.410 --> 00:27:36.860
or even who our target audience
is, to the training data set

00:27:36.860 --> 00:27:39.630
that we curate, all
of those decisions

00:27:39.630 --> 00:27:42.410
then cascade and radiate
down to circumscribe

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:45.980
the set of decisions that
an end user is able to make.

00:27:45.980 --> 00:27:48.088
Because of that, and
because we are all

00:27:48.088 --> 00:27:49.880
human at the end of
the day, we will always

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:53.990
sustain some amount of bias
for or against something

00:27:53.990 --> 00:27:55.220
or whatever.

00:27:55.220 --> 00:27:57.680
And so it's on us to continually
be vigilant about that

00:27:57.680 --> 00:27:59.425
and ask questions
about how can we

00:27:59.425 --> 00:28:01.550
be more mindful about those
decisions we're making,

00:28:01.550 --> 00:28:03.320
that then radiate
down to the end user.

00:28:03.320 --> 00:28:04.700
MARK MIRCHANDANI: If only there
were some kind of guidebook

00:28:04.700 --> 00:28:06.020
to help people decide.

00:28:06.020 --> 00:28:07.327
MARK MANDEL: If only there was.

00:28:07.327 --> 00:28:07.910
DI DANG: Yeah.

00:28:07.910 --> 00:28:09.368
MARK MANDEL: So
naturally segueing,

00:28:09.368 --> 00:28:11.758
so if people want to learn
more, where can they go?

00:28:11.758 --> 00:28:13.550
DI DANG: Yes, I'm very
glad that you asked.

00:28:13.550 --> 00:28:14.930
We just finalized our URL.

00:28:14.930 --> 00:28:17.330
So if people want to
check out the guidebook,

00:28:17.330 --> 00:28:21.860
they can go to
Design.google/AI-guidebook.

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:23.540
You'll see the six
chapters there,

00:28:23.540 --> 00:28:25.755
along with the worksheets
and interactive exercises

00:28:25.755 --> 00:28:28.130
that kind of help you dig
into the material in a more

00:28:28.130 --> 00:28:29.660
actionable way.

00:28:29.660 --> 00:28:32.000
You also see across the
site a feedback button

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:34.842
that allows you to fill
out a form of any thoughts

00:28:34.842 --> 00:28:36.800
or concerns that you
have, to share it with us,

00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:39.830
so that we can continue
improving this document

00:28:39.830 --> 00:28:42.140
and improving the set
of best practices.

00:28:42.140 --> 00:28:44.600
The reality of it is
that the field of AI

00:28:44.600 --> 00:28:47.060
is quickly advancing
and quickly changing.

00:28:47.060 --> 00:28:49.400
And so should the way that
we design our products

00:28:49.400 --> 00:28:51.890
as a result. And as
a result of that, so

00:28:51.890 --> 00:28:54.350
should this set of
best practices and this

00:28:54.350 --> 00:28:56.000
guidance itself.

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:57.660
And so, I want to hear from you.

00:28:57.660 --> 00:28:59.810
We want to hear from you.

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:02.550
I'll also say that my
MDs are open on Twitter.

00:29:02.550 --> 00:29:04.370
I'm at dqpdang.

00:29:04.370 --> 00:29:07.560
Feel free to reach out any
time, if you feel the need to,

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:09.703
outside of the
feedback form itself.

00:29:09.703 --> 00:29:11.870
And hopefully, I'll see you
at an event or something

00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:12.375
otherwise.

00:29:12.375 --> 00:29:14.375
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I do
want to ask one question.

00:29:14.375 --> 00:29:14.930
MARK MANDEL: Uh-oh.

